  "notes": "Alabama-lineage band country: a soul-inflected lead over a tight ensemble that alternates two-beat dance cuts with close-harmony midtempos. This is the section's cut-time entry and that is the reason it exists — \"on the dance cuts a 16th-note snare stream at a ~130 notated tempo (i.e. the cut-time reading of the archetype) with a two-beat kick, hat on quarters\". So the snare keeps the parent's inherited `train_16ths` stream, `swing.grid` stays 16th at a flat 0.50, and `bpm.mode` is 130 inside the stated 74–132 span; the catalogue \"sits lower than its neotraditional peers, and the two-beat is used at a walking rather than a running tempo\". Harmony is the stated I–IV–V and I–V–vi–IV \"with a IV-chord dwell at the top of choruses\". The identity device is stated as an ensemble fact rather than a production one — \"four-part band harmony ... the backing voices are a fixed ensemble rather than overdubbed stacks, and they enter on the chorus in close intervals\" — which is the countermelody's octave_echo share at `densityRatio` 0.55. The bass carries the entry's own explanation of the band's feel: \"note values longer than the fast-honky-tonk norm, which is why the band reads as groove rather than drive\", i.e. the loosest `followRootsProb` and highest `anticipationProb` of the radio wing here. ⚠ `confidence` is low, and the research is explicit about why: \"only three catalogue readings were reachable\" (132, 95, 74) and \"no production documentation was found\". The entry rates itself medium-low and says it is included because the 130-notated cut-time reading fills a real gap in the archetype's tempo map; that is the whole justification for this model and it is repeated here. ⚠ INFERRED as a consequence: the velocity tiers, the −1 ms snare offset, `percs` contents, and every melody, countermelody and bassline number.",
